From 191b080fba37275296dd34de3fa8994b4b3e0c02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:14:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] feat(control-plane): deploy inference services when a
 launcher is set

Until now the app injected neither a workflow runtime nor a launcher, so
every service stopped at WorkflowRuntimeUnavailable. With
FORGEMESH_RUNTIME_LAUNCHER set (together with FORGEMESH_INVENTORY_FILE)
the lifespan now assembles the deployment loop:

- the launcher is built from the environment next to the inventory,
  before any database is opened, so bad settings refuse the start;
- the workflow runs live in their own SQLite file next to the registry
  (workflow_db_path, e.g. forgemesh.workflows.db);
- recover() resumes unfinished deployments, then mark_lost_instances()
  degrades Ready services whose instance is gone, and only then does
  the controller start, with the runtime, a DeploymentReleaser and the
  launcher injected into the reconciler;
- shutdown unwinds controller -> runtime -> launcher -> workflow store,
  so compensations that run while the runtime closes can still stop
  instances.

POST /workflows keeps answering 503: it would start step-less runs in
the deployment store, under keys a tenant could choose.

forgemesh-workflow-runtime becomes a dependency of the control plane
and is installed by its Dockerfile. The poll intervals become module
constants so the end-to-end test can shorten them.

tests/e2e/test_closed_loop.py drives the real app with a one-device
inventory and the fake launcher: create -> Ready -> streaming chat to
the advertised endpoint -> delete (instance stopped, allocation
released, resource gone) -> re-create on the same device, and a restart
that turns the Ready service Degraded with Ready=False InstanceLost.
